"...'Oh, that I might live for a day, I would attend to the Blessed One's teaching; much indeed would be done by me'—the monk who develops mindfulness of death in this way;
'Oh, that I might live for the duration of eating one meal, I would attend to the Blessed One's teaching; much indeed would be done by me'—the monk who develops mindfulness of death in this way;
'Oh, that I might live for the duration of chewing and swallowing four or five morsels, I would attend to the Blessed One's teaching; much indeed would be done by me'—the monk who develops mindfulness of death in this way;
These monks, monks, are said to live heedlessly; they develop mindfulness of death sluggishly for the destruction of the taints.

But, monks, the monk who develops mindfulness of death in this way: 'Oh, that I might live for the duration of chewing and swallowing just one morsel of food, I would attend to the Blessed One's teaching; much indeed would be done by me';
And the monk who develops mindfulness of death in this way: 'Oh, that I might live for the duration of breathing in and out, or breathing out and in, I would attend to the Blessed One's teaching; much indeed would be done by me';
These monks, monks, are said to live heedfully; they develop mindfulness of death keenly for the destruction of the taints.

Therefore, monks, you should train yourselves thus: 'We will live heedfully; we will develop mindfulness of death keenly for the destruction of the taints.'
Thus, monks, should you train yourselves."
The Ninth Sutta.

## 10. Second Maraṇassati Sutta
20. On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Nātika in the Brick House. There the Blessed One addressed the monks:
"Monks, mindfulness of death, when developed and cultivated, is of great fruit and great benefit; it merges into the Deathless, it ends in the Deathless. And how, monks, is mindfulness of death developed and cultivated so that it is of great fruit and great benefit, merges into the Deathless, and ends in the Deathless?

Here, monks, when the day has passed and night has fallen, a monk reflects thus:
'Many are the causes of my death: a snake might bite me, a scorpion might sting me, or a centipede might sting me. Because of that...'
